# Target C-II - every reflection module is exact-diameter anchored or entirely active-free

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the shallow-tooth reflection branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** Type-II full-core slow-scale reflection modules at `eta=3/26`  
**Dependencies:** slow-scale central reflection braid; simultaneous reflection block diagonalization; radial-interface loaded-sink theorem; full-source actual-diameter midpoint localization; convex diameter-thrackle property  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_module_anchor_dichotomy_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The radial-interface theorem is stronger module-by-module than the canonical-head split used in the first loaded-head corollary. For one reflection module

\[
U_i=\{z\in A:|z-q_i|>D\},
\]

every component of `G_C[U_i]` either contains a positively diameter-loaded vertex or is one source point with

\[
\deg_C=\deg_H=0.
\]

Therefore each whole module has an exact dichotomy:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{ANCHORED:}&U_i\text{ contains an actual-diameter endpoint};\\[1mm]
\text{FREE:}&\text{every blocker }z\in U_i\text{ has }\deg_C(z)=\deg_H(z)=0.
\end{array}}
\tag{MA.1}
\]

In an ANCHORED module, choose any loaded blocker `z_i in U_i` and one actual diameter partner `t_i`. The full-source midpoint theorem, together with the already proved opposite-shore localization of `q_i` and `U_i`, gives

\[
\boxed{|t_i-q_i|=O(D^{3/4}).}
\tag{MA.2}
\]

It also gives

\[
\boxed{
\angle(z_i-t_i,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}).
}
\tag{MA.3}
\]

The slow-scale module spacing is much larger:

\[
\operatorname{dist}(q_i,q_j),
\operatorname{dist}(U_i,U_j)
=\Omega(\kappa_DD^{3/4}),
\qquad
\kappa_D\to\infty.
\]

Hence **all anchored modules simultaneously**, with no normal-cone averaging and no constant-factor spacing loss, yield pairwise vertex-disjoint actual diameter edges. Their directions inherit the module-normal separation

\[
\Omega(\kappa_DD^{-1/4}),
\]

so the convex diameter-thrackle property makes them an exact crossing braid whose entire crossing set lies in one

\[
\boxed{B(o,O(D/\kappa_D))=B(o,o(D))}
\tag{MA.4}
\]

central disk.

Thus the full slow-scale family of

\[
(c_*-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D},
\qquad c_*=0.129220199818177\ldots,
\]

modules has the stronger source-wide alternative

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{EXACT-BRAID:}&\Theta(D^{1/4}/\kappa_D)\text{ tagged actual diameters}\n
&\text{are vertex-disjoint and pairwise crossing in one }O(D/\kappa_D)\text{ core};\\[1mm]
\text{FREE-MODULE:}&\Theta(D^{1/4}/\kappa_D)\text{ modules have every upper blocker}\n
&\text{contact-isolated and actual-diameter-isolated in the original source.}
\end{array}}
\tag{MA.5}
\]

This supersedes the weaker loaded-head extraction: anchoring by **any** blocker in the module is enough, and the exact diameter partner is automatically localized near the reflected apex at the universal `D^(3/4)` scale.

---

## 1. Module-level dichotomy

Fix one selected reflection module `(q_i,U_i)`. The radial-interface theorem proved:

1. `G_C[U_i]` is a disjoint union of angularly monotone paths and isolated vertices;
2. every nontrivial component contains a vertex of `B_+`;
3. a singleton component with any contact to the complement of `U_i` also lies in `B_+`;
4. a component containing no `B_+` vertex is therefore a single full-contact isolate;
5. because the relative-interior stress has the full actual diameter graph in its positive support, that full-contact isolate also has diameter degree zero.

If some component contains `B_+`, the module is ANCHORED.

If no component contains `B_+`, every component is one point `z` satisfying

\[
\boxed{
\deg_C(z)=\deg_H(z)=0.
}
\tag{MA.6}

Since the components partition `U_i`, this proves the FREE side of (MA.1) for **every blocker** in the module, not merely for the canonical farthest head.

---

## 2. Any loaded blocker determines an exact diameter partner near `q_i`

Suppose module `i` is ANCHORED. Choose

\[
z_i\in U_i\cap B_+.
\]

Because `z_i` is positively diameter-loaded, choose an actual diameter partner `t_i`:

\[
|z_i-t_i|=D.
\tag{MA.7}
\]

Let

\[
m_i={z_i+t_i\over2}
\]

be its midpoint. The full-source geometry theorem gives one common circumcenter `o` with

\[
\boxed{|m_i-o|=O(D^{3/4})}
\tag{MA.8}
\]

uniformly over every actual diameter edge.

The simultaneous reflection theorem gives, uniformly over the full blocker set,

\[
|z_i-o|={D\over2}+O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
\angle(z_i-o,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}),
\tag{MA.9}
\]

while the reflected apex satisfies

\[
|q_i-o|={D\over2}+O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
\angle(q_i-o,-n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}).
\tag{MA.10}
\]

Equations (MA.9)--(MA.10) imply the vector cancellation

\[
\boxed{
(z_i-o)+(q_i-o)=O(D^{3/4}).
}
\tag{MA.11}
\]

Indeed the angular errors contribute `O(D*D^-1/4)=O(D^3/4)` and the radial errors contribute only `O(sqrt D)`.

Since

\[
t_i-o=2(m_i-o)-(z_i-o),
\]

we have

\[
\begin{aligned}
t_i-q_i
&=2(m_i-o)-(z_i-o)-(q_i-o).
\end{aligned}
\]

Using (MA.8) and (MA.11),

\[
\boxed{|t_i-q_i|=O(D^{3/4}).}
\tag{MA.12}
\]

No convex-normal averaging is needed.

---

## 3. The exact diameter direction inherits the module normal

From

\[
z_i-t_i=2(z_i-m_i)
\]

and (MA.8)--(MA.9),

\[
z_i-t_i
=D n_i+O(D^{3/4})
\tag{MA.13}
\]

after orienting `n_i` toward the blocker shore. Dividing by the exact length `D` gives

\[
\boxed{
\angle(z_i-t_i,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}).
}
\tag{MA.14}

Thus the actual diameter direction is automatically aligned to the reflection module phase.

For the selected slow-scale family, distinct module normals satisfy

\[
\angle(n_i,n_j)
\ge(2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4}.
\tag{MA.15}

Because `kappa_D->infinity`, (MA.14) is negligible relative to (MA.15). Hence distinct anchored-module diameter directions obey

\[
\boxed{
\angle(z_i-t_i,z_j-t_j)
\ge(2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4}.
}
\tag{MA.16}

All normals lie in one quadrant, so these are ordinary lifted angular gaps and preserve the module order.

---

## 4. All anchored-module diameter edges are vertex-disjoint

The blocker sets `U_i` are pairwise disjoint, so all chosen heads `z_i` are distinct.

The simultaneous block-diagonal theorem gives

\[
\boxed{
|q_i-q_j|=\Omega(\kappa_DD^{3/4})
\qquad(i\ne j).
}
\tag{MA.17}

By (MA.12), every partner `t_i` lies in an `O(D^3/4)` ball about `q_i`. Since `kappa_D->infinity`, those partner balls are pairwise disjoint for sufficiently large `D`. Therefore all `t_i` are distinct.

A cross-collision `t_i=z_j` is impossible as well. Equations (MA.9)--(MA.10), together with the fact that all selected normals lie in one quadrant, give

\[
|q_i-z_j|\ge(1/\sqrt2-o(1))D.
\tag{MA.18}
\]

But (MA.12) is `o(D)`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\{z_i,t_i\}\cap\{z_j,t_j\}=\varnothing
\qquad(i\ne j).
}
\tag{MA.19}

No thinning beyond the original slow-scale module selection is needed.

---

## 5. Anchored modules form an exact central diameter braid

The actual diameter graph is a convex geometric thrackle. By (MA.19), every two selected anchored-module diameter edges are vertex-disjoint, so they cross properly.

Their midpoint locations satisfy (MA.8), while their pairwise line-angle gaps satisfy (MA.16). If `c_ij` is the intersection of two supporting lines, the elementary line-intersection estimate gives

\[
|c_{ij}-m_i|
\le {O(D^{3/4})\over
\sin(\Omega(\kappa_DD^{-1/4}))}.
\]

Because `kappa_D=o(D^1/4)`, the angle tends to zero and its sine is comparable to the angle. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
|c_{ij}-o|=O(D/\kappa_D).
}
\tag{MA.20}

Since `kappa_D->infinity`, this is `o(D)`. The diameter segments themselves cross, so the line intersections are the actual proper crossing points.

Thus every anchored subfamily is an exact actual-diameter braid through one common `O(D/kappa_D)` core, with one braid edge tagged by each anchored reflection module.

---

## 6. Source-wide anchored/free split

The full slow-scale family has

\[
M_D>(c_*-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
\tag{MA.21}
\]

modules. Partition them into ANCHORED and FREE according to (MA.1). One class contains at least `M_D/2` modules.

Hence either:

### EXACT-BRAID

At least

\[
\boxed{
(c_*/2-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
}
\tag{MA.22}

modules contribute vertex-disjoint actual diameter edges. Every two cross properly, their direction gaps are `Omega(kappa_D D^-1/4)`, their opposite endpoints lie `O(D^3/4)` from the corresponding reflected apexes, and all crossings lie in one `O(D/kappa_D)` central disk.

or:

### FREE-MODULE

At least

\[
\boxed{
(c_*/2-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
}
\tag{MA.23}

modules satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\deg_C(z)=\deg_H(z)=0
\quad\text{for every }z\in U_i.
}
\tag{MA.24}

Thus the **entire** upper-diameter obstruction set of each retained module consists of original-source points carrying neither an old unit contact nor an old actual diameter edge.

For any subpolynomial divergent `kappa_D=D^{o(1)}`, both surviving populations have size

\[
D^{1/4-o(1)}.
\]

Taking `kappa_D=log log D`, for example, forces either an exact tagged diameter braid of size `Theta(D^1/4/log log D)` through an `O(D/log log D)` core, or the same-order family of modules whose complete blocker sets are active-constraint-free.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The reflection branch can no longer hide behind arbitrary contact propagation or an arbitrary attachment of its upper blockers to the diameter graph.

```text
one selected reflection module (q_i,U_i)
    |
    +-- ANCHORED:
    |      some z_i in U_i is diameter-loaded
    |      choose |z_i-t_i|=D
    |      t_i is automatically O(D^(3/4)) from q_i
    |      direction(z_i t_i) is O(D^-1/4) from n_i
    |
    +-- FREE:
           every z in U_i has deg_C(z)=deg_H(z)=0
```

Across the slow-scale family, the anchored half is automatically a full-size exact diameter braid; no head-specific normal-cone averaging and no extra constant-spacing extraction are needed.

C-II remains open. The two resulting local targets are now sharply separated:

1. **EXACT-BRAID:** combine the exact diameter endpoints `z_i,t_i` with the loaded tooth bases and penetration tags. In particular the opposite endpoint `t_i` is forced into the `O(D^3/4)` reflected-apex neighborhood while the diameter direction follows the ordered module normals.
2. **FREE-MODULE:** perform a finite compensated reflection when **every** diameter violator of `q_i` is an old contact/diameter isolate. Any first obstruction created while moving those blocker isolates must be genuinely new, rather than inherited from the canonical active graph.

**Disposition:** upgraded the radial loaded-sink theorem to a module-level dichotomy. Either a positive fraction of the slow-scale modules produces a full-size, vertex-disjoint exact actual-diameter braid through the same `o(D)` central core, or a positive fraction has its entire upper-blocker set contact- and diameter-isolated. C-II and Level I remain open.
